# systemverilog_preprocessor.ebnf
#
# The SystemVerilog preprocessor's directive-STRUCTURE grammar (IEEE 1800-2017 / 1800-2023 clause 22).
# Scope:
# - Every clause-22 directive (§22.1's list): `define` / `undef` / `undefineall`, `include` (a quoted,
#   angle-bracketed or macro-valued path), the `ifdef` family, `timescale`, `default_nettype`,
#   `celldefine` / `endcelldefine`, `resetall`, `unconnected_drive` / `nounconnected_drive`, `pragma`,
#   `line`, `begin_keywords` / `end_keywords` (SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.1, release 1.0.8)
# - Nested conditional-compilation blocks
# - Macro formal/default argument syntax
# - Macro body primitives: token-paste, stringize, the `\`" escaped quote, backslash-newline
#   continuation, string literals and comments as opaque text
# - A text macro usage anywhere a source line may hold one, the start of a line included
#   (`__FILE__` / `__LINE__` among them, §22.13)
#
# - Per-edition PROFILES (SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.9, release 1.0.9): `sv_2017` (the default), `sv_2023`,
#   `sv_2023_tolerant` (as `sv_2023` for every directive) and `verilog_2005`, the SystemVerilog family's own profile
#   names — each derives its edition's directive forms and refuses the others'.
#
# Notes:
# - Directive STRUCTURE, deterministic. Non-directive text is captured as passthrough text, LEXED (release 1.0.9:
#   strings, comments, escaped identifiers and macro usages are lexemes, so a directive word inside one is text and
#   an unclosed string or comment is refused — the lexical conventions hold in every group, IEEE 1800 §22.6).
# - A directive may begin anywhere on a line (IEEE 1800-2023 §22.2), except `include and `line, which own theirs.
# - LIVENESS is not decided here. Whether a conditional group is compiled depends on the compilation (command-line
#   symbols, earlier files, includes); the expansion stage decides it. This parser parses every group's structure,
#   and inside a conditional group records a line that does not parse as a `malformed_directive` item (§22.6: an
#   ignored group need only be lexically valid); at the top level, live in every compilation, it refuses it.
#
# WS-DIRECTIVE.2 — grammar-level layout policy: the preprocessor is
# line-oriented, so its REGEX TOKENS are whitespace-sensitive (a token like
# `[^\r\n]*` must start matching at the cursor, not after an auto-skip that
# could swallow a significant newline). String terminals and trailing layout
# keep the default skip. (This directive replaced the retired engine-side
# grammar-NAME gate; the policy now lives here, in the grammar, per the
# EBNF-single-source-of-truth doctrine.)

@whitespace_sensitive: { regex_tokens: true }
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.9: the SystemVerilog family's editions, spelled as grammars/systemverilog.ebnf spells them;
# an unspecified profile reads IEEE 1800-2017, as the expansion stage's unprofiled reading does.
@profile_alias: { "2017": sv_2017, "ieee1800-2017": sv_2017, "ieee_1800_2017": sv_2017 }
@profile_alias: { "2023": sv_2023, "ieee1800-2023": sv_2023, "ieee_1800_2023": sv_2023 }
@profile_alias: { "1364-2005": verilog_2005, "ieee1364-2005": verilog_2005, "ieee_1364_2005": verilog_2005 }
@default_profile: sv_2017
@entry: true
systemverilog_preprocessor_file := pp_item*
-> {type: "systemverilog_preprocessor_file", items: $1}

# STIMULI-SIGNOFF.12 — generation-side lexical cohesion: pp_item is a LINE
# construct whose trailing newline is optional (`pp_define := ... newline?`),
# so two STACKED quantified renderings can fuse into one line (a define body
# eats to end-of-line and would swallow the next directive). The stimuli
# generator therefore inserts "\n" between adjacent pp_item renderings unless
# the junction already carries a line break — and "\r\n" also counts as one,
# because THIS grammar's own newline token is `/\r?\n/`. (This directive
# replaced the retired engine-side grammar-NAME + container-rule-name gate;
# the policy now lives here, in the grammar, per the EBNF-single-source-of-
# truth doctrine. Parsing is unaffected: the directive steers stimuli only.)
@quantified_separator: { insert: "\n", satisfied_by: ["\n", "\r\n"] }
pp_item := pp_define              -> {kind: "define",              body: $1}
         | pp_undef               -> {kind: "undef",               body: $1}
         | pp_include             -> {kind: "include",             body: $1}
         | pp_timescale           -> {kind: "timescale",           body: $1}
         | pp_default_nettype     -> {kind: "default_nettype",     body: $1}
         | pp_celldefine          -> {kind: "celldefine"}
         | pp_endcelldefine       -> {kind: "endcelldefine"}
         | pp_resetall            -> {kind: "resetall",            body: $1}
         | pp_undefineall         -> {kind: "undefineall",         body: $1}
         | pp_unconnected_drive   -> {kind: "unconnected_drive",   body: $1}
         | pp_nounconnected_drive -> {kind: "nounconnected_drive", body: $1}
         | pp_pragma              -> {kind: "pragma",              body: $1}
         | pp_line                -> {kind: "line",                body: $1}
         | pp_begin_keywords      -> {kind: "begin_keywords",      body: $1}
         | pp_end_keywords        -> {kind: "end_keywords",        body: $1}
         | pp_conditional         -> {kind: "conditional",         body: $1}
         | pp_non_directive_line  -> {kind: "non_directive_line",  body: $1}
         | pp_blank_line          -> {kind: "blank_line"}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Core directives
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# §22.5.1: "If formal arguments are used, the list of formal argument names shall be enclosed in parentheses
# immediately following the name of the macro" — so a `(` right after the name ALWAYS opens the list: a list that
# does not parse makes the define malformed, never object-like (SVPP-0013: through 1.0.8 it fell back to an
# object-like define with the text `(typ,` — UVM's uvm_declare_type_alias(TYPE,NAME,SFX=) among them). A define
# ends at its line end (define_end), never mid-line.
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — the formal list follows a SIMPLE name: §22.5.1 puts it "immediately following the name
# of the macro", and an escaped name ends at white space (A.9.3), so `macro_name`'s escaped form never derived a list
# here (the parser could not follow it with `(`); 1800-2023's own escaped-name form is `pp_define_escaped_formals`.
# Through the first 1.0.9 build the alternative named `macro_name`, and generation rendered the underivable form.
pp_define := pp_define_escaped_formals
           | kw_define macro_simple_name macro_formals macro_body? define_end
          -> {name: $2, formals: $3, body: $4}
           | kw_define macro_object_name !formals_open macro_body? define_end
          -> {name: $2, formals: [], body: $4}
pp_undef := kw_undef undef_name directive_comment_tail newline?
         -> {name: $2, comment: $3}
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.4 (SVPP-0007) — §22.4 (both editions): "Only white space or a comment may appear on
# the same line as the `include compiler directive". The former `newline?` let the line's remainder re-read
# as a second, passthrough item (`include "a.sv" x was accepted); the line must end here.
pp_include := kw_include include_path directive_comment_tail line_end
           -> {path: $2, comment: $3}
# Each parameter may also be a macro usage — a defined macro "can be used anywhere" (1364-2005 §19.3.1,
# 1800-2017 / 1800-2023 §22.5.1; 1800-2023 §22.2 states the directive case) — and the whole specification may be
# one (`timescale `TS). The same holds for every fixed-syntax directive's parameters below.
pp_timescale := kw_timescale timescale_value p_slash timescale_value directive_comment_tail newline?
             -> {unit: $2, precision: $4, comment: $5}
              | kw_timescale p_macro_usage directive_comment_tail newline?
             -> {macro: $2, comment: $3}
pp_default_nettype := kw_default_nettype nettype_param directive_comment_tail newline?
                   -> {nettype: $2, comment: $3}
pp_celldefine := kw_celldefine directive_comment_tail newline?
              -> {comment: $2}
pp_endcelldefine := kw_endcelldefine directive_comment_tail newline?
                 -> {comment: $2}

# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.1 (release 1.0.8) — the seven clause-22 directives the grammar did not derive.
# Each carries its body at the `pp_item` dispatch (the `pp_undef` precedent), so no comment is dropped.
#   `resetall            §22.3     `undefineall          §22.5.3 (no arguments)
#   `unconnected_drive   §22.9     pull0 | pull1, and `nounconnected_drive
#   `pragma              §22.11, Syntax 22-8: pragma_name [ pragma_expression { , pragma_expression } ]
#   `line                §22.12, Syntax 22-9: number "filename" level
#   `begin_keywords      §22.14, Syntax 22-10: "version_specifier", and `end_keywords
# A directive with a DEFINED end keeps `newline?`: 1800-2023 §22.2 lets "each directive whose syntax shows a
# defined end to the directive ... be followed by another valid language element on the same line".
# `pragma` has no defined end (§22.2 names it among the directives "that include an indeterminate amount of
# text"), so its line must END there: `line_end` is required, and `pragma p a,` is refused.
pp_resetall := kw_resetall directive_comment_tail newline?
            -> {comment: $2}
@profiles: ["sv_2017", "sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
pp_undefineall := kw_undefineall directive_comment_tail newline?
               -> {comment: $2}
pp_unconnected_drive := kw_unconnected_drive drive_param directive_comment_tail newline?
                     -> {drive: $2, comment: $3}
pp_nounconnected_drive := kw_nounconnected_drive directive_comment_tail newline?
                       -> {comment: $2}
pp_pragma := kw_pragma pragma_name pragma_expression_list? directive_comment_tail line_end
          -> {name: $2, expressions: $3, comment: $4}
# §22.12: "only white space may appear on the same line as the `line directive. Comments are not allowed on
# the same line as a `line directive. All parameters in the `line directive are required." — every separator is
# `line_space` (spaces and tabs only, never `inline_trivia`, which admits a block comment), and the line ends
# after the level.
pp_line := kw_line line_number line_filename line_level line_terminator
        -> {number: $2, filename: $3, level: $4}
pp_begin_keywords := kw_begin_keywords version_param directive_comment_tail newline?
                  -> {version: $2, comment: $3}
pp_end_keywords := kw_end_keywords directive_comment_tail newline?
                -> {comment: $2}

# Syntax 22-8. `pragma_keyword = pragma_value` is tried before the bare keyword, and a bare identifier reads
# as a keyword; an identifier VALUE is reachable after `=`. clause 34's own examples separate the
# expressions with commas, as the syntax box does.
pragma_name := identifier
pragma_keyword := identifier
pragma_expression_list := pragma_expression (comma pragma_expression)*
                       -> [$1, $2::2*]
pragma_expression := pragma_keyword assign pragma_value -> {kind: "keyword_value", keyword: $1, value: $3}
                   | pragma_keyword                     -> {kind: "keyword", keyword: $1}
                   | pragma_value                       -> {kind: "value", value: $1}
pragma_value := pragma_value_list -> {kind: "list", expressions: $1}
              | pragma_number     -> {kind: "number", text: $1}
              | quoted_string     -> {kind: "string", text: $1}
              | identifier        -> {kind: "identifier", name: $1}
pragma_value_list := lparen pragma_expression_list rparen
                  -> $2
# A.8.7 `number`: a real (fixed point or exponent form), a sized or unsized based integral number
# (`4'b10_1x`, `8 'sd 255`, `'hFF`), or an unsigned decimal — the real forms first, so `3.5e-2` is one token.
pragma_number := directive_trivia /[0-9][0-9_]*\.[0-9][0-9_]*(?:[eE][+-]?[0-9][0-9_]*)?|[0-9][0-9_]*[eE][+-]?[0-9][0-9_]*|(?:[1-9][0-9_]*[ \t]*)?'[sS]?(?:[dD][ \t]*(?:[0-9][0-9_]*|[xXzZ?]_*)|[bB][ \t]*[01xXzZ?][01xXzZ?_]*|[oO][ \t]*[0-7xXzZ?][0-7xXzZ?_]*|[hH][ \t]*[0-9a-fA-FxXzZ?][0-9a-fA-FxXzZ?_]*)|[0-9][0-9_]*/

# §22.12: the number "shall be a positive integer", the filename "shall be a string literal", the level
# "shall be 0, 1, or 2".
line_space := space_or_tab*
line_number := line_space /0*[1-9][0-9]*/
            | line_macro_usage
line_filename := line_space /"([^"\\\r\n]|\\.)*"/
              | line_macro_usage
line_level := line_space /[012]\b/
           | line_macro_usage
line_terminator := line_space line_end
# The end of input as its own rule (`end_of_input`), so generation reads it as the anchor it is (SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16).
line_end := /\r?\n/
          | end_of_input

# Syntax 22-10. One grammar serves both editions: the list is 1800-2023's (1800-2017's plus "1800-2023"),
# which §22.14 permits — "Implementations and other standards are permitted to extend the `begin_keywords
# directive with custom version specifiers" — and the expansion stage holds the per-edition admission
# (`keyword_versions.rs`, W_SVPP_KEYWORDS_VERSION_NOT_IN_EDITION). Any other text is refused.
# Each edition's own Syntax 22-10 / 19-10 list; an unrecognized specifier "shall be an error" (§22.14).
version_specifier := version_specifier_2023
                   | version_specifier_2017
                   | version_specifier_2005
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
version_specifier_2023 := p_layout /"(?:1800-2023|1800-2017|1800-2012|1800-2009|1800-2005|1364-2005|1364-2001-noconfig|1364-2001|1364-1995)"/
@profiles: ["sv_2017"]
version_specifier_2017 := p_layout /"(?:1800-2017|1800-2012|1800-2009|1800-2005|1364-2005|1364-2001-noconfig|1364-2001|1364-1995)"/
@profiles: ["verilog_2005"]
version_specifier_2005 := p_layout /"(?:1364-2005|1364-2001-noconfig|1364-2001|1364-1995)"/

include_path := quoted_string       -> {kind: "quoted", text: $1}
              | angle_path          -> {kind: "angle",  text: $1}
              | include_macro_usage -> {kind: "macro",  body: $1}

# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.7 — §22.5.1 (both editions): "The `include directive can be followed by a macro,
# instead of a string literal" (`include `home(myfile)). The actuals are one balanced-parenthesis span
# (`$text`); `include `else stays refused — a directive keyword is not a macro.
include_macro_usage := !directive_keyword macro_reference macro_usage_arguments?
                    -> {name: $2, arguments: $3}
macro_usage_arguments := lparen macro_argument_atom* rparen
                      -> $text
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 (SVPP-0015) — §22.5.1 (1800-2017 and 1800-2023): "Actual arguments and defaults shall not
# contain comma or right parenthesis characters outside matched pairs of left and right parentheses (), square
# brackets [], braces {}, double quotes "", or an escaped identifier" — and a comment is a comment. Through the
# first 1.0.9 build the actuals were `[^()\r\n]+` runs, so `include `M(")") was refused and a `)` inside a comment
# closed the list. A string, a block comment and an escaped identifier are atoms; inside [ ] or { } a parenthesis is
# a character; a bracket or brace no pair closes is a character too.
macro_argument_atom := macro_argument_text
                     | macro_argument_group
                     | macro_argument_square
                     | macro_argument_curly
                     | macro_argument_lexeme
                     | macro_argument_stray
macro_argument_group := lparen macro_argument_atom* rparen
macro_argument_square := /\[/ macro_argument_in_square* /\]/
macro_argument_curly := /\{/ macro_argument_in_curly* /\}/
macro_argument_in_square := macro_argument_text | macro_argument_square | macro_argument_curly
                          | macro_argument_lexeme | /[(){}]/
macro_argument_in_curly := macro_argument_text | macro_argument_square | macro_argument_curly
                         | macro_argument_lexeme | /[()\[\]]/
macro_argument_lexeme := macro_argument_string
                       | directive_block_comment
                       | macro_argument_escaped
                       | macro_argument_slash
macro_argument_text := /[^()\[\]{}"\\\/\r\n]+/
macro_argument_string := /"(?:[^"\\\r\n]|\\[^\r\n])*"/
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — an escaped identifier is TERMINATED by white space: escaped_identifier ::= \ { any
# printable ASCII character except white space } white_space (A.9.3, all three editions; §5.6.1). The regex takes
# every non-white-space character, so the parser already holds it; the restriction declares it for GENERATION,
# which otherwise rendered `\a` + `(x)` as `\a(x)`, one identifier.
[>! /\S/]
macro_argument_escaped := /\\[^ \t\r\n]*/
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — a `/` that a `/` or `*` follows is not this token but a comment opener (1800-2017 §5.4;
# the lexer reads the longest token). The parser already holds it (the layout before it takes the comment); the
# restriction declares it for GENERATION, which otherwise rendered `/` + `/*c*/` as `//*c*/`, one line comment.
[>! "/", "*"]
macro_argument_slash := /\// !comment_opener_tail
macro_argument_stray := /[\[\]{}]/

# Syntax 22-7 default_nettype_value (identical in all three editions); through 1.0.8 any identifier passed.
nettype_value := p_nettype_word -> {kind: "identifier", body: $1}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Conditional-compilation blocks
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

pp_conditional := pp_if_branch pp_elsif_branch* pp_else_branch? pp_endif
               -> {if_branch: $1, elsif_branches: $2, else_branch: $3}

# pp_if_keyword: the `ifdef`/`ifndef` polarity, lifted into a NAMED rule
# (the systemverilog.ebnf `binary_operator` idiom). An inline alternation
# `(kw_ifdef | kw_ifndef)` as the lead element of pp_if_branch corrupted
# the positional model, so `keyword:$1` emitted `<invalid_sequence_access>`
# (SVPP-0001 / inline-alternation-$N defect class). The named rule fixes
# it and gives consumers a clean `{kind:"ifdef"|"ifndef"}` polarity
# discriminator.
pp_if_keyword := kw_ifdef  -> {kind: "ifdef"}
               | kw_ifndef -> {kind: "ifndef"}

# The condition: a text_macro_identifier or a macro usage in every edition; 1800-2023's `( ifdef_macro_expression )`
# under sv_2023 (if_condition). `elsif takes the same condition (1800-2017: a text_macro_identifier only; through
# 1.0.8 its condition ran to the line end).
pp_if_branch := pp_if_keyword if_condition conditional_tail? newline? pp_group_item*
             -> {keyword: $1, macro: $2, tail: $3, items: $5}
pp_elsif_branch := kw_elsif if_condition conditional_tail? newline? pp_group_item*
                -> {macro: $2, tail: $3, items: $5}
pp_else_branch := kw_else conditional_tail? newline? pp_group_item*
               -> {tail: $2, items: $4}
pp_endif := kw_endif conditional_tail? newline?
         -> {tail: $2}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Macro declaration support
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Cat-A fix (POST-SV-AUDIT.2.1): a pure comma-separated list. The old
# `{first:$2, rest:$3}` exposed the raw `[[comma,formal],…]` iteration
# envelope (consumers had to walk past every comma). The canonical
# extraction-spread `[$2, $3::2*]` (the `object_properties` reference
# idiom in return_annotation.ebnf) drops the separator and yields a
# clean flat `macro_formal` list.
#
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.5 (SVPP-0008) — §22.5.1 (2017): "The left parenthesis shall follow the text macro name
# immediately, with no space in between" (2023: "no white space shall separate the opening parenthesis from the
# macro name"). `formals_open` is a regex token, so nothing is skipped before it: `define M (x) y is an
# OBJECT-like macro whose text is "(x) y" — the reading the expansion stage has always had — where the former
# `lparen` (inline trivia first) made it function-like.
# §22.5.1: "The formal argument names shall be simple_identifiers, separated by commas and optionally white
# space" — no comment there (SVPP-EXPANSION.5(o)); a newline is white space under 1364-2005 / 1800-2017 (the line
# discipline). An empty default (`b=)`) is allowed (1800-2017 S17:146-150); formal defaults are IEEE 1800 only.
macro_formals := formals_open macro_formal (formal_comma macro_formal)* formal_rparen
              -> [$2, $3::2*]
macro_formal := formal_name formal_default?
             -> {name: $1, default: $2}
@profiles: ["sv_2017", "sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
formal_default := formal_assign macro_default_value?

macro_default_value := macro_default_atom+
                    -> {atoms: $1}
macro_default_atom := macro_token_paste -> {kind: "token_paste"}
                    | macro_stringize   -> {kind: "stringize"}
                    | macro_reference   -> {kind: "macro_reference", body: $1}
                    | macro_default_text -> {kind: "text",  body: $1}
                    | macro_default_paren_group
                    | question          -> {kind: "question"}
                    | colon             -> {kind: "colon"}

# GRAMMAR-WELLFORMED.H.9 (SVPP-0004): IEEE 1800 §22.5.1 — a macro default may contain a
# comma or right parenthesis only INSIDE "a balanced pair of parentheses". The former
# bare `lparen`/`rparen` alternatives above let the possessive `macro_default_atom+`
# swallow the FORMALS' closing `)` (so a default on the LAST formal — legal, used by
# the LRM's own examples — re-routed the whole `(...)` into the macro body with
# `formals: []`). A balanced group consumes only its own parentheses, so an unbalanced
# `)` correctly terminates the default; commas are legal inside the group (and only
# there), per the same LRM sentence. Found by the H.7.2 plannable-rule reach pass
# (the parsed-but-routed-elsewhere verdict on `macro_default_text`).
macro_default_paren_group := lparen macro_default_group_atom* rparen
                          -> {kind: "paren_group", atoms: $2}
macro_default_group_atom := macro_default_atom -> $1
                         | comma -> {kind: "comma"}

# Comment-aware (SV-EXH-PROOF.2.3.1, PGEN-SV-EXH-PROOF-0008): a run of
# allowed chars, but a `/* ... */` block comment is matched ATOMICALLY
# (alternative tried first) so a backtick *inside* a comment no longer
# splits the run. Without this the bare `[^`...]` char-class swallowed
# a comment's `/*` then halted at the comment's internal backtick,
# wrongly rejecting valid SV like ``\`define X(a=/*\`*/) y``. Mirrors
# the proven systemverilog.ebnf `timeunit_separator_trivia` /
# `block_comment` idiom; accepts strictly more, narrows nothing; the
# `{kind:"text", body:$1}` AST shape is unchanged.
#
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.6 (SVPP-0009) — a string literal is one atom of default text: §22.5.1 "Actual arguments
# and defaults shall not contain comma or right parenthesis characters outside matched pairs of ... double
# quotes", so `define M(a="x,y") closes its formals.
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 (with SVPP-0013) — the leading layout is white space only: a comment belongs to the default's
# text, which reads it atomically. With `inline_trivia` in front, a default that is ONLY a comment
# (`define X(a=/*`*/) y — the SV-EXH-PROOF.2.3.1 example) lost the comment to the layout and left the run nothing
# to match; release 1.0.8 accepted it only through SVPP-0013's object-like fallback (`formals: []`).
macro_default_text := space_or_tab_opt macro_default_chunk
# Its comment crosses a line under 1800-2017 only (1800-2023 §22.2, as `block_comment`).
macro_default_chunk := macro_default_chunk_multiline
                     | macro_default_chunk_line
@profiles: ["verilog_2005", "sv_2017"]
macro_default_chunk_multiline := /(?:\/\*([^*]|\*+[^*\/])*\*+\/|"(?:[^"\\\r\n]|\\.)*"|[^`(),?:\r\n])+/
macro_default_chunk_line := /(?:\/\*(?:[^*\r\n]|\*+[^*\/\r\n])*\*+\/|"(?:[^"\\\r\n]|\\.)*"|[^`(),?:\r\n])+/
space_or_tab_opt := space_or_tab*

macro_body := macro_body_fragment+
           -> {fragments: $1}
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.3 (SVPP-0006) — backslash-newline CONTINUATION: §22.5.1 (2017) "If more than one line
# is necessary to specify the text, the newline shall be preceded by a backslash"; (2023) "immediately
# preceded". The body formerly ended at the first newline, its tree holding `"a + \\"` and the continuation
# reading as a separate passthrough line. A backslash followed by white space is NOT a continuation
# (SVPP-EXPANSION.5(r): no edition licenses it).
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.6 — `\`" (§22.5.1: "The `define macro text can also include `", `\`", and ``").
macro_body_fragment := macro_line_continuation -> {kind: "line_continuation"}
                     | macro_token_paste -> {kind: "token_paste"}
                     | macro_stringize   -> {kind: "stringize"}
                     | macro_escaped_quote -> {kind: "escaped_quote"}
                     | macro_directive_word -> {kind: "directive", body: $1}
                     | macro_reference   -> {kind: "macro_reference", body: $1}
                     | macro_body_text   -> {kind: "text",   body: $1}
                     | macro_lparen      -> {kind: "lparen"}
                     | macro_rparen      -> {kind: "rparen"}
                     | macro_comma       -> {kind: "comma"}
                     | macro_question    -> {kind: "question"}
                     | macro_colon       -> {kind: "colon"}

# Comment-aware — same fix + rationale as macro_default_text above
# (SV-EXH-PROOF.2.3.1, PGEN-SV-EXH-PROOF-0008).
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.6 (SVPP-0009) — three more atoms, each per §22.5.1: a `//` comment ("the comment shall
# not become part of the substituted text"), which runs up to — not including — a continuation backslash
# (2023: "the comment ends before the backslash and the macro text continues on the next line"); a plain string
# literal ("Macro substitution and argument substitution shall not occur within string literals"), so a
# backtick, a parenthesis or a comma inside it no longer splits or ends the run; and a backslash, consumed only
# with the character after it, so the backslash that ends a line is the continuation's.
# Macro text: a block comment may cross a line only where each newline is preceded by a backslash under
# 1364-2005 / 1800-2017, anywhere under 1800-2023 (S23:175-179). A `/` that opens no comment is a character.
macro_body_text := macro_body_text_line
                | macro_body_text_2023
@profiles: ["verilog_2005", "sv_2017"]
macro_body_text_line := macro_trivia macro_text_run_line
@profiles: ["verilog_2005", "sv_2017"]
macro_text_run_line := (macro_text_chunk_line | macro_text_slash)+ -> $text
@profiles: ["verilog_2005", "sv_2017"]
macro_text_chunk_line := /(?:\/\*(?:[^*\\\r\n]|\\\r?\n|\\[^\r\n]|\*+(?:[^*\/\\\r\n]|\\\r?\n|\\[^\r\n]))*\*+\/|\/\/(?:[^\r\n\\]|\\(?:[^\r\n]|\z))*|"(?:[^"\\\r\n]|\\(?:[^\r\n]|\z))*"|\\(?:[^\r\n]|\z)|[^`(),?:\r\n\\\/])+/
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
macro_body_text_2023 := macro_trivia macro_text_run_2023
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
macro_text_run_2023 := (macro_text_chunk_2023 | macro_text_slash)+ -> $text
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
macro_text_chunk_2023 := /(?:\/\*([^*]|\*+[^*\/])*\*+\/|\/\/(?:[^\r\n\\]|\\(?:[^\r\n]|\z))*|"(?:[^"\\\r\n]|\\(?:[^\r\n]|\z))*"|\\(?:[^\r\n]|\z)|[^`(),?:\r\n\\\/])+/
macro_line_continuation := macro_trivia /\\\r?\n/
macro_reference := macro_reference_simple
                 | macro_reference_escaped
macro_reference_simple := macro_trivia /`[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_$]*/
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — an escaped identifier is TERMINATED by white space: escaped_identifier ::= \ { any
# printable ASCII character except white space } white_space (A.9.3, all three editions; §5.6.1). The regex takes
# every non-white-space character, so the parser already holds it; the restriction declares it for GENERATION,
# which otherwise rendered `\a` + `(x)` as `\a(x)`, one identifier.
[>! /\S/]
macro_reference_escaped := macro_trivia /`(?:\\[^ \t\r\n`][^ \t\r\n]*|\\`(?:[^ \t\r\n"][^ \t\r\n]*)?)/
@profiles: ["sv_2017", "sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
macro_token_paste := macro_trivia /``/
@profiles: ["sv_2017", "sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
macro_stringize := macro_trivia /`"/
@profiles: ["sv_2017", "sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
macro_escaped_quote := macro_trivia /`\\`"/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Passthrough lines
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.2 (SVPP-0005) — a line may OPEN with a text macro usage (§22.5.1 `text_macro_usage
# ::= `text_macro_identifier [ ( list_of_actual_arguments ) ]` may stand wherever source text may):
# `uvm_object_utils(c), `ASSERT(...), `__FILE__ (§22.13). The former first character `[^`\r\n]` refused every
# such line — 4,182 of the 4,477 corpus files release 1.0.7 rejected. `!directive_keyword` keeps a MALFORMED
# directive refused (`timescale 1ns, a stray `else), which would otherwise re-read as a macro-usage line;
# `{text: $2}` because the lookahead occupies the first slot.
pp_non_directive_line := !directive_keyword non_directive_text text_run_end
                      -> {text: $2}
pp_blank_line := newline
              -> {kind: "blank"}
non_directive_text := inline_trivia text_run
# GRAMMAR-WELLFORMED.H.5.3 — WITNESSING sample steering. The older `@sample: " "` short-circuited
# the whole rule to a space, so the inner `line_comment?` was never expanded and `line_comment`
# was never WITNESSED. `@sample: " //"` emits a minimal valid line comment (`line_comment :=
# /\/\/[^\r\n]*/` accepts `//` with an empty body), so on re-parse `inline_trivia line_comment?`
# matches `//` → `line_comment` is genuinely exercised → a real reachability witness, while
# staying benign (no free-content over-generation).
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — the sample is WITHDRAWN: a rule-level sample renders the rule's text and nothing
# after it, so the item that followed landed inside the `//` (`` `pragma p // `endif ``). `line_comment` now
# declares that it ends its line, and ordinary generation witnesses it.
directive_comment_tail := inline_trivia line_comment?

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identifiers and literals
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The macro name (SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.8): simple or ESCAPED (Annex A identifier, every edition), and never a
# compiler directive's name — "it shall be illegal to redefine a compiler directive as a macro name" (§22.5.1),
# each edition's own directive list (1364-2005 has no `undefineall / `__FILE__ / `__LINE__).
macro_name := macro_simple_name
            | macro_escaped_name
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — an OBJECT-like macro's name is not followed by `(`: §22.5.1, a `(` immediately after
# the name opens the formal list. `!formals_open` holds it for the parser; the restriction declares it for
# GENERATION, which rendered a body opening with `(` against the name (`define lEe(... read as a formal list).
[>! /\(/]
macro_object_name := macro_name
macro_simple_name := directive_trivia !directive_name_word /[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_$]*/
                  -> [$1, $3]
# An escaped macro name is terminated by white space too (A.9.3); its generation-side separator is
# `macro_object_name`'s, the only rule that reaches `macro_name` (the formal-list alternative takes
# `macro_simple_name`, the 1800-2023 escaped-formals form `macro_escaped_formals_name`) — so no `[>! … ]` line of its
# own sits here, directly after a rule that ends in a return annotation: the self-hosted EBNF reader (the
# dual-run's arm 2) cannot read one there (SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.20).
macro_escaped_name := directive_trivia /\\[^ \t\r\n]+/
# The same name where 1800-2023's single terminating white space is the grammar's own next element
# (`escaped_name_end`), so no separator is declared.
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
macro_escaped_formals_name := directive_trivia /\\[^ \t\r\n]+/
directive_name_word := directive_name_word_sv
                     | directive_name_word_2005
@profiles: ["sv_2017", "sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
directive_name_word_sv := /(?:__FILE__|__LINE__|begin_keywords|celldefine|default_nettype|define|else|elsif|end_keywords|endcelldefine|endif|ifdef|ifndef|include|line|nounconnected_drive|pragma|resetall|timescale|unconnected_drive|undef|undefineall)\b/
@profiles: ["verilog_2005"]
directive_name_word_2005 := /(?:begin_keywords|celldefine|default_nettype|define|else|elsif|end_keywords|endcelldefine|endif|ifdef|ifndef|include|line|nounconnected_drive|pragma|resetall|timescale|unconnected_drive|undef)\b/
identifier := directive_trivia /[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_$]*/
bt_identifier := bt_simple_identifier
               | bt_escaped_identifier
bt_simple_identifier := inline_trivia /`[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_$]*/
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — an escaped identifier is TERMINATED by white space: escaped_identifier ::= \ { any
# printable ASCII character except white space } white_space (A.9.3, all three editions; §5.6.1). The regex takes
# every non-white-space character, so the parser already holds it; the restriction declares it for GENERATION,
# which otherwise rendered `\a` + `(x)` as `\a(x)`, one identifier.
[>! /\S/]
bt_escaped_identifier := inline_trivia /`\\[^ \t\r\n]+/

# §5.9: a string on one line unless the newline is preceded by a backslash (through 1.0.8 a raw newline passed).
quoted_string := directive_trivia /"(?:[^"\\\r\n]|\\(?:\r?\n|[^\r\n]))*"/
@profiles: ["sv_2017", "sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
angle_path := directive_trivia /<[^>\r\n]+>/
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Trivia and punctuation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

inline_trivia := (space_or_tab | block_comment)*
space_or_tab := /[ \t]+/
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — a `//` comment ends its line (1800-2017 §5.4): nothing follows it there. The regex already
# takes the rest of the line; the restriction declares it for GENERATION, which otherwise put the next item (an
# `` `endif ``) on the comment's line.
[>! /\S/, / /, /\t/]
line_comment := /\/\/[^\r\n]*/
block_comment := /\/\*([^*]|\*+[^*\/])*\*+\//
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — the layout INSIDE a directive (between its tokens): a comment there crosses a line only
# where the edition lets a directive cross one. 1364-2005 and 1800-2017 have no one-line rule; 1800-2023 §22.2:
# "Unless specified below as supporting multiple lines, compiler directives shall be all on one line". Through the
# first 1.0.9 build every directive's interior used `inline_trivia`, whose comment crosses newlines, so
# `timescale 1ns /* a<newline> b */ / 1ps passed `sv_2023` against this grammar's own edition matrix. Before a
# directive, after its last token, and in passthrough or macro text a comment keeps `inline_trivia` (a first cut
# made `block_comment` itself one-line under 1800-2023 and moved 2,066 corpus trees that hold such comments, and
# refused iverilog's br_gh782a.v — `include "f" /* a comment continued on the next line */).
directive_trivia := (space_or_tab | directive_block_comment)*
directive_block_comment := block_comment_multiline
                         | block_comment_line
@profiles: ["verilog_2005", "sv_2017"]
block_comment_multiline := /\/\*([^*]|\*+[^*\/])*\*+\//
block_comment_line := /\/\*(?:[^*\r\n]|\*+[^*\/\r\n])*\*+\//

newline := inline_trivia /\r?\n/

assign := directive_trivia /=/
comma := directive_trivia /,/
colon := directive_trivia /:/
question := directive_trivia /\?/
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
bang := directive_trivia /!/
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
logical_or := directive_trivia /\|\|/
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
logical_and := directive_trivia /&&/
# Punctuation is a REGEX terminal (SVPP-0014): a string terminal keeps the default layout skip, which crosses
# newlines and comments — through 1.0.8 a `define body ran on into the next line when that line opened with
# ( ) , ? : and a comment between formals was skipped.
lparen := directive_trivia /\(/
formals_open := /\(/   # no leading trivia: §22.5.1, see macro_formals
rparen := directive_trivia /\)/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Directive keywords
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# GRAMMAR-WELLFORMED.H.5.1.2 — each directive keyword regex ends in `\b`, asserting a word
# boundary must follow (so `` `define `` is one token, never `` `define `` fused with a following
# `macro_name`). The `[>! /\w/]` lexical-annotation (LEXICAL-ANNOTATIONS Obligation C — the
# follow-restriction construct built for the stimuli generator) declares this for GENERATION:
# the generator self-terminates the keyword's surface with a space so it can never fuse with the
# next word char (`` `define_CV ``/`` `ifndefR7Sh `` would re-parse-fail the `\b`). `[>! …]` is a
# documented parse-direction no-op, so the generated parser is unchanged.
[>! /\w/]
kw_define := inline_trivia /`define\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_undef := inline_trivia /`undef\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_include := inline_trivia /`include\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_ifdef := inline_trivia /`ifdef\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_ifndef := inline_trivia /`ifndef\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_elsif := inline_trivia /`elsif\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_else := inline_trivia /`else\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_endif := inline_trivia /`endif\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_timescale := inline_trivia /`timescale\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_default_nettype := inline_trivia /`default_nettype\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_celldefine := inline_trivia /`celldefine\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_endcelldefine := inline_trivia /`endcelldefine\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_resetall := inline_trivia /`resetall\b/
[>! /\w/]
@profiles: ["sv_2017", "sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
kw_undefineall := inline_trivia /`undefineall\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_unconnected_drive := inline_trivia /`unconnected_drive\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_nounconnected_drive := inline_trivia /`nounconnected_drive\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_pragma := inline_trivia /`pragma\b/
[>! /\w/]
# `line takes white space only before it (§22.12); `kw_line_word` is the trivia-tolerant spelling the
# `directive_keyword` lookahead needs, so `/* c */ `line 1 "f" 0` stays refused rather than re-reading as text.
kw_line := line_space /`line\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_line_word := inline_trivia /`line\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_begin_keywords := inline_trivia /`begin_keywords\b/
[>! /\w/]
kw_end_keywords := inline_trivia /`end_keywords\b/
# The twenty §22.1 directive keywords (`__FILE__` / `__LINE__` are predefined MACROS, §22.13) — read only by
# the negative lookaheads of `pp_non_directive_line` and `include_macro_usage`. A new directive keyword joins
# this list in the same edit, or a malformed use of it would be accepted as a macro-usage line.
directive_keyword := kw_define | kw_undef | kw_undefineall | kw_include
                   | kw_ifdef | kw_ifndef | kw_elsif | kw_else | kw_endif
                   | kw_timescale | kw_default_nettype | kw_celldefine | kw_endcelldefine
                   | kw_resetall | kw_unconnected_drive | kw_nounconnected_drive
                   | kw_pragma | kw_line_word | kw_begin_keywords | kw_end_keywords


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Release 1.0.9 — the lexically-aware text run, directives anywhere on a line, and a group line that does not
# parse recorded as an item (SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.10 / .11 / .12; ledger SVPP-0010 – SVPP-0012).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Inside a conditional group a line that does not parse is RECORDED, not refused: the group may be ignored in a
# compilation, and an ignored group "shall still follow the ... lexical conventions" and nothing more (1800-2017
# §22.6; 1800-2023 says it of every ignored block_of_text). The line is still lexed, so an unclosed string or
# comment refuses the file. The conditional keywords are never recorded so: they delimit the groups.

# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — the same generation-side cohesion as `pp_item` (STIMULI-SIGNOFF.12): two stacked group
# items are separated by a line break unless the junction carries one.
@quantified_separator: { insert: "\n", satisfied_by: ["\n", "\r\n"] }
@branch_policy: ordered
pp_group_item := pp_item
               | pp_malformed_directive -> {kind: "malformed_directive", body: $1}

pp_malformed_directive := !conditional_keyword group_lexical_line text_run_end
                       -> {text: $2}
group_lexical_line := group_lexeme+ -> $text
group_lexeme := text_lexeme
              | group_directive_word
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — the word that makes a group line malformed is a DIRECTIVE's: any other backtick word is
# a macro usage, which `text_lexeme` already reads (`text_macro_usage`), so the line's accepted set is unchanged; the
# conditional keywords delimit the groups and are never in a line. Through the first 1.0.9 build this was any
# backtick word, so generation drew macro usages — valid lines `pp_item` takes — and never witnessed this rule.
group_directive_word := kw_define | kw_undef | kw_undefineall | kw_include
                      | kw_timescale | kw_default_nettype | kw_celldefine | kw_endcelldefine
                      | kw_resetall | kw_unconnected_drive | kw_nounconnected_drive
                      | kw_pragma | kw_line_word | kw_begin_keywords | kw_end_keywords
conditional_keyword := kw_ifdef | kw_ifndef | kw_elsif | kw_else | kw_endif

# A conditional directive keeps a trailing COMMENT as its `tail` (`endif // FOO); any other text that follows it on
# its line is the group's first item — 1800-2023 §22.6: the block_of_text ends at the next `elsif / `else / `endif and
# "White space is not considered when determining termination" (SVPP-0012: through 1.0.8 the text was the tail, or
# after `elsif part of the condition).
conditional_tail := inline_trivia line_comment

# A passthrough run ends at a line end, at the end of input, or before a directive that may stand mid-line (1800-2023
# §22.2: "A language element is allowed on the same line before a directive"); `include (§22.4) and `line (§22.12)
# own their line in every edition.
text_run_end := newline
              | &mid_line_directive
              | end_of_input
mid_line_directive := !kw_include !kw_line_word directive_keyword
end_of_input := /\z/

# The lexemes of passthrough text: a block comment (it may span lines), a line comment, a string (one line, or
# continued by a backslash-newline, §5.9), a triple-quoted string (1800-2023), an escaped identifier (to the next white
# space; A.9.3 allows the empty one), a text macro usage, and other characters. A directive keyword outside them ends
# the run; inside them it is text (1800-2023 §22.2: "Directives are not recognized within comments or string
# literals"; SVPP-0010, SVPP-0011). The run is one string in the tree (`$text`), as the 1.0.8 line was.
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — a run's FIRST lexeme is never a block comment: `non_directive_text := inline_trivia
# text_run`, and the leading layout takes every comment that opens the run (`block_comment` and `text_block_comment`
# are one regex), in every parse. Stated, it moves no verdict and no tree; generation then places a comment after a
# lexeme, the only place the parser reads one as a `text_block_comment` (its forced witness was a lone comment the
# layout took, so the rule was UNKNOWN wherever the sample did not happen to put one mid-run).
text_run := text_run_head text_lexeme* -> $text
text_run_head := text_triple_string
               | text_line_comment
               | text_string
               | text_escaped_identifier
               | text_macro_usage
               | text_chars
               | text_slash
text_lexeme := text_block_comment
             | text_triple_string
             | text_line_comment
             | text_string
             | text_escaped_identifier
             | text_macro_usage
             | text_chars
             | text_slash
text_block_comment := /\/\*([^*]|\*+[^*\/])*\*+\//
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — a `//` comment ends its line (1800-2017 §5.4): nothing follows it there. The regex already
# takes the rest of the line; the restriction declares it for GENERATION, which otherwise put the next item (an
# `` `endif ``) on the comment's line.
[>! /\S/, / /, /\t/]
text_line_comment := /\/\/[^\r\n]*/
text_string := /"(?:[^"\\\r\n]|\\(?:\r?\n|[^\r\n]))*"/
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — an escaped identifier is TERMINATED by white space: escaped_identifier ::= \ { any
# printable ASCII character except white space } white_space (A.9.3, all three editions; §5.6.1). The regex takes
# every non-white-space character, so the parser already holds it; the restriction declares it for GENERATION,
# which otherwise rendered `\a` + `(x)` as `\a(x)`, one identifier.
[>! /\S/]
text_escaped_identifier := /\\[^ \t\r\n]*/
text_macro_usage := !directive_keyword bt_identifier
text_chars := /(?:[^`"\\\/\r\n]|\/[^\/*`"\\\r\n])+/
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — a `/` that a `/` or `*` follows is not this token but a comment opener (1800-2017 §5.4;
# the lexer reads the longest token). The parser already holds it (the layout before it takes the comment); the
# restriction declares it for GENERATION, which otherwise rendered `/` + `/*c*/` as `//*c*/`, one line comment.
[>! "/", "*"]
text_slash := /\// !comment_opener_tail
comment_opener_tail := /[\/*]/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Release 1.0.9 — directive parameters, conditions, formals and macro names per edition
# (SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.8 / .9 / .13 / .14; ledger SVPP-0013, SVPP-0014).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The LINE DISCIPLINE. 1800-2023 §22.2: "Unless specified below as supporting multiple lines, compiler directives
# shall be all on one line." 1364-2005 and 1800-2017 have no such sentence, so a directive's tokens may be separated
# by newlines and comments there (iverilog's br_gh782b.v writes a `timescale one token per line).
p_layout := p_layout_multiline
          | directive_trivia
@profiles: ["verilog_2005", "sv_2017"]
p_layout_multiline := (space_or_tab | block_comment | p_line_comment_newline | p_newline)*
p_line_comment_newline := /\/\/[^\r\n]*\r?\n/
p_newline := /\r?\n/

p_identifier := p_layout /[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_$]*/
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — a `/` that a `/` or `*` follows is not this token but a comment opener (1800-2017 §5.4;
# the lexer reads the longest token). The parser already holds it (the layout before it takes the comment); the
# restriction declares it for GENERATION, which otherwise rendered `/` + `/*c*/` as `//*c*/`, one line comment.
[>! "/", "*"]
p_slash := p_layout /\//
p_unsigned_number := p_layout /[0-9][0-9_]*/
p_time_unit := p_layout /(s|ms|us|ns|ps|fs)\b/
p_time_literal := p_unsigned_number p_time_unit
               -> {value: $1, unit: $2}
p_nettype_word := p_layout /(?:wire|tri0|tri1|triand|trior|trireg|tri|wand|wor|uwire|none)\b/
p_kw_pull0 := p_layout /pull0\b/
p_kw_pull1 := p_layout /pull1\b/

# A macro usage in a parameter position (a defined macro "can be used anywhere" — every edition).
p_macro_usage := !p_directive_word p_bt_identifier macro_usage_arguments?
              -> {kind: "macro_usage", name: $2, arguments: $3}
p_directive_word := p_directive_word_common
                  | p_directive_word_sv
p_directive_word_common := p_layout /`(?:define|undef|include|ifdef|ifndef|elsif|else|endif|timescale|default_nettype|celldefine|endcelldefine|resetall|unconnected_drive|nounconnected_drive|pragma|line|begin_keywords|end_keywords)\b/
p_bt_identifier := p_bt_simple_identifier
                 | p_bt_escaped_identifier
p_bt_simple_identifier := p_layout /`[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_$]*/
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — an escaped identifier is TERMINATED by white space: escaped_identifier ::= \ { any
# printable ASCII character except white space } white_space (A.9.3, all three editions; §5.6.1). The regex takes
# every non-white-space character, so the parser already holds it; the restriction declares it for GENERATION,
# which otherwise rendered `\a` + `(x)` as `\a(x)`, one identifier.
[>! /\S/]
p_bt_escaped_identifier := p_layout /`\\[^ \t\r\n]+/
line_macro_usage := line_space !p_directive_word p_bt_identifier macro_usage_arguments?
                 -> {kind: "macro_usage", name: $3, arguments: $4}

timescale_value := p_time_literal
                 | p_macro_usage
nettype_param := nettype_value
               | p_macro_usage
drive_param := p_pull_drive
             | p_macro_usage
p_pull_drive := p_kw_pull0 -> {kind: "pull0"}
              | p_kw_pull1 -> {kind: "pull1"}
version_param := version_specifier
               | p_macro_usage
undef_name := p_identifier
            | p_macro_usage
condition_name := p_identifier
                | p_macro_usage

# The formal list: white space only between its tokens (§22.5.1 "separated by commas and optionally white
# space" — a comment there is refused, SVPP-EXPANSION.5(o)), newlines included under 1364-2005 / 1800-2017.
formal_ws := (space_or_tab | formal_newline)*
@profiles: ["verilog_2005", "sv_2017"]
formal_newline := /\r?\n/
formal_name := formal_ws /[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_$]*/
formal_comma := formal_ws /,/
formal_rparen := formal_ws /\)/
formal_assign := formal_ws /=/
@profiles: ["sv_2017", "sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
p_directive_word_sv := p_layout /`undefineall\b/
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
text_triple_string := /"""(?:[^"\\]|\\[\s\S]|"(?:[^"\\]|\\[\s\S])|""(?:[^"\\]|\\[\s\S]))*"""/
if_condition := condition_name
              | ifdef_paren_expression
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
ifdef_paren_expression := lparen ifdef_implication rparen
                       -> {kind: "expression", expression: $2}
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
ifdef_implication := ifdef_or implication_op ifdef_implication -> {kind: "binary", operator: $2, left: $1, right: $3}
                   | ifdef_or -> $1
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
ifdef_or := ifdef_and logical_or ifdef_or -> {kind: "binary", operator: "||", left: $1, right: $3}
          | ifdef_and -> $1
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
ifdef_and := ifdef_unary logical_and ifdef_and -> {kind: "binary", operator: "&&", left: $1, right: $3}
           | ifdef_unary -> $1
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
ifdef_unary := bang ifdef_unary -> {kind: "not", operand: $2}
             | ifdef_primary -> $1
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
ifdef_primary := lparen ifdef_implication rparen -> {kind: "group", expression: $2}
               | identifier -> {kind: "identifier", name: $1}
               | p_macro_usage -> $1
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
implication_op := directive_trivia /<->/ -> "<->"
                | directive_trivia /->/ -> "->"
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
pp_define_escaped_formals := kw_define macro_escaped_formals_name escaped_name_end macro_formals macro_body? define_end
                          -> {name: $2, formals: $4, body: $5}
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
escaped_name_end := /[ \t]/
macro_directive_word := macro_trivia macro_directive_word_text
macro_directive_word_text := macro_directive_word_sv
                           | macro_directive_word_2005
@profiles: ["sv_2017", "sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
macro_directive_word_sv := /`(?:begin_keywords|celldefine|default_nettype|define|else|elsif|end_keywords|endcelldefine|endif|ifdef|ifndef|include|line|nounconnected_drive|pragma|resetall|timescale|unconnected_drive|undef|undefineall)\b/
@profiles: ["verilog_2005"]
macro_directive_word_2005 := /`(?:begin_keywords|celldefine|default_nettype|define|else|elsif|end_keywords|endcelldefine|endif|ifdef|ifndef|include|line|nounconnected_drive|pragma|resetall|timescale|unconnected_drive|undef)\b/
# SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16 — a `/` that a `/` or `*` follows is not this token but a comment opener (1800-2017 §5.4;
# the lexer reads the longest token). The parser already holds it (the layout before it takes the comment); the
# restriction declares it for GENERATION, which otherwise rendered `/` + `/*c*/` as `//*c*/`, one line comment.
[>! "/", "*"]
macro_text_slash := /\// !comment_opener_tail
# §22.5.1: the macro text runs to the first newline not preceded by a backslash, so a define ends at a line end —
# never mid-line, where an unlexable remainder (an unclosed comment) would otherwise re-read as a following item.
define_end := newline
           | macro_trivia end_of_input   # SV-CORPUS-GRAD.13c.2v.16: white space or a comment may end the file
# Macro TEXT's own layout: a block comment there may span lines only under IEEE 1800-2023 (S23:175-179: "If the
# macro text contains a multi-line block comment, the newline characters in the block comment are not required to be
# preceded by a backslash"); 1364-2005 / 1800-2017 end the macro text at the first newline not preceded by one.
macro_trivia := (space_or_tab | macro_block_comment)*
macro_block_comment := macro_block_comment_line
                     | macro_block_comment_2023
@profiles: ["verilog_2005", "sv_2017"]
macro_block_comment_line := /\/\*(?:[^*\\\r\n]|\\\r?\n|\\[^\r\n]|\*+(?:[^*\/\\\r\n]|\\\r?\n|\\[^\r\n]))*\*+\//
@profiles: ["sv_2023", "sv_2023_tolerant"]
macro_block_comment_2023 := /\/\*([^*]|\*+[^*\/])*\*+\//
macro_lparen := macro_trivia /\(/
macro_rparen := macro_trivia /\)/
macro_comma := macro_trivia /,/
macro_question := macro_trivia /\?/
macro_colon := macro_trivia /:/
